-- Carnap's default `prop` propositional language, as the truth-table
-- exercise type speaks it: ASCII connectives only, atoms are single Roman
-- letters of either case, five separate precedence rungs (negation, then
-- ∧ over ∨ over → over ↔), every rung chains, and plain parentheses may
-- enclose anything.
--
-- Carnap's own spec. Ported from the worked example of the same name in
-- @aufbau/syntax and free to diverge from it, as its sibling explains.
--
-- The `@syntax role` annotations are what let the truth-table type read a
-- parse without knowing that this book calls conjunction `and`: the library
-- has no connective set of its own, by design, and a role is the channel it
-- offers instead. See `logic/specs/roles.ts`.
--
-- The atoms are ordinary nullary declarations — MM0's finite vocabulary. The
-- hand parser this replaced also read a bare-digit subscript (`P0`, `R12`),
-- which no signature can spell; it went when the truth-table type swapped onto
-- this file (2026-08-24), deliberately. A course that wants `P1` back, or
-- wants `Rain` outright, declares it in an `aufbau-mm0` block that extends
-- this file — which is possible here only because of the next paragraph.

-- The surface delimiter set: where one chunk of student input ends and the
-- next begins, fixed before anything is looked up. Every operator spelling is
-- listed, so `P->Q` reads without spaces and so does `~~P`. Nothing here may
-- be a string the vocabulary cannot answer to, and no lexicon name may be
-- split by it; the reader checks both.
--
-- **The letters are deliberately not listed** (2026-08-28). They were, and it
-- cost the whole multi-character lexicon: a delimiter is a property of a
-- string, so declaring `P` splits `Rain` into `R a i n` and the reader refuses
-- the name. What the letters bought here was nothing — the claim in this
-- comment used to be that they are what makes `~~P` read tight, but `~` is
-- itself a delimiter and bounds its own operand. A language with a quantifier
-- is the case where they earn their keep, because `AxF(x)` has no punctuation
-- in `Ax` for anything else to break on; see forallx: Calgary, which pays the
-- one-character price for exactly that and where a multi-character name has to
-- declare itself a delimiter to be readable.
--| @syntax delimiter $ ( ) ~ /\ \/ -> <-> $
delimiter $ ( ) $;

provable sort wff;

term A: wff;
term B: wff;
term C: wff;
term D: wff;
term E: wff;
term F: wff;
term G: wff;
term H: wff;
term I: wff;
term J: wff;
term K: wff;
term L: wff;
term M: wff;
term N: wff;
term O: wff;
term P: wff;
term Q: wff;
term R: wff;
term S: wff;
term T: wff;
term U: wff;
term V: wff;
term W: wff;
term X: wff;
term Y: wff;
term Z: wff;
term a: wff;
term b: wff;
term c: wff;
term d: wff;
term e: wff;
term f: wff;
term g: wff;
term h: wff;
term i: wff;
term j: wff;
term k: wff;
term l: wff;
term m: wff;
term n: wff;
term o: wff;
term p: wff;
term q: wff;
term r: wff;
term s: wff;
term t: wff;
term u: wff;
term v: wff;
term w: wff;
term x: wff;
term y: wff;
term z: wff;

--| @syntax role negation
term not (p: wff): wff;
prefix not: $~$ prec 50;

--| @syntax role conjunction
term and (p q: wff): wff;
infixl and: $/\$ prec 40;

--| @syntax role disjunction
term or (p q: wff): wff;
infixl or: $\/$ prec 35;

--| @syntax role conditional
term imp (p q: wff): wff;
infixr imp: $->$ prec 30;

--| @syntax role biconditional
term iff (p q: wff): wff;
infixl iff: $<->$ prec 25;
